# 351-KRN-0040 — `init` gives `keystore` a VFS client with its own badge — done

**Type:** kernel (init) · **Owner:** `KRN` track · **Priority:** P3 · **Status:** done (2026-10-09) · **Blocked by:** — · **Main task:** [351](../issues/351-self-update.md) · **Constitution:** MC-11.9, MC-3.3

Done for the network track's [351-NET-0005](351-NET-0005-persistent-device-key.done) by the storage session, in the kernel track's area with the maintainer's permission (2026-10-09). Numbered from 0040 so as not to meet the kernel session's own numbers.

## Problem

The key service keeps the device key across boots in its private directory of the boot disk, `system/keystore`. `vfs_server` lets only the badge `mind::fs::BADGE_KEYSTORE` open it. Only `init` mints badged clients of a service for the services it starts.

## Plan

- `init` gives `keystore` a client of `vfs_server` badged `BADGE_KEYSTORE`, in `SLOT_VFS` (slot 3).
- No other service or program gets that badge.

## Acceptance criteria

`keystore` reads and writes `system/keystore`; the shell and programs cannot open it.

## Related

[351-NET-0005](351-NET-0005-persistent-device-key.done), `docs/profile/bootstrap.md`.

## Resolution

Done on 2026-10-09:
- `mind::fs::BADGE_KEYSTORE` (2) and `BADGE_NETPOLICY` (3), the badges of the private directories in `system/`.
- `init` gives `keystore` a `vfs_server` client badged `BADGE_KEYSTORE` in `SLOT_VFS`. No one else gets that badge.

The `tls` suite's device key check (351-NET-0005) shows `keystore` writing its directory and the shell refused it, on x86 and aarch64.
